#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063962 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e901ea71-aeab-5e95-939e-2401558aa708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Extrema of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "69180c3b-7c26-5e5f-8d7a-42aed98431bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-3736" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of $f(x) = 2x - x^2$  on the interval $[-1,4].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=2x-x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 2-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n2-2x = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=1.\n$$\n\nThe critical points are $x=1$ and the endpoints $x=-1,4$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2(-1)-(-1)^{2}=-3 \\\\ f(1) & =2(1)-(1)^{2}=1 \\\\ f(4) & =2(4)-(4)^{2}=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(1)=1,$ so the global maximum value is $1.$\n\n- The least value is $f(4)=-8,$ so the global minimum value is $-8.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/c277beb3423cc80d3ac2a001ac1063059b645c65fc8cb77978050f3831274b31.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063963 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2106ea68-f48f-5372-87c0-fa8126509655" :knowledge-point/title "Discarding Stationary Points of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "a0f47cd2-ea8f-5278-ab67-46ecd65d2f4d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3737" :question/problem "Find the global maximum and minimum values for the function $f(x) = x^4 - 6 x^2$ on the closed interval $[-1, 3].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x^4 - 6x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 4x^3 - 12x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}-12x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{2}-3) & =0 \\\\ 4x(x+\\sqrt{3})(x-\\sqrt{3}) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x = -\\sqrt 3 \\approx -1.73$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,3]$, we ignore this point.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0,\\sqrt{3}$ and the endpoints $x=-1,3$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at the critical points:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tf(-1) \t& = (-1)^4 - 6(-1)^2 = -5\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(0) \t\t& = (0)^4 - 6(0)^2 = 0\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(\\sqrt 3) \t& = (\\sqrt 3)^4 - 6(\\sqrt 3)^2 = -9\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(3) \t\t& = (3)^4 - 6(3)^2 = 27 \\\t\t\n \t\t}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the global maximum is $27$ and the global minimum is $-9.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/74ce5114dbc93035b0be078be12149cced66ce881dfbc37768ed9febcdfcba87.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2106ea68-f48f-5372-87c0-fa8126509655" :knowledge-point/title "Discarding Stationary Points of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25d86cfd-3823-5e43-a83b-0971d6221498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25d86cfd-3823-5e43-a83b-0971d6221498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316024} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3729fc4-3797-5340-81aa-57fda3329d71" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9641cbff-6df9-5810-96c4-dcfb71465c80" :question/math-academy-id "q-314729" :question/problem "The exact global minimum value of the function $f(x)=2{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+1$ on the closed interval $-1\\le x\\le 1$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=2{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =8{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}(2x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,1],$ the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2(-1)^{4}-4(-1)^{3}+1=2+4+1=7 \\\\ f(0) & =2(0)^{4}-4(0)^{3}+1=1 \\\\ f(1) & =2(1)^{4}-4(1)^{3}+1=2-4+1=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=7,$ so the global maximum is $7.$\n\n- The least value is $f(1)=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/fa4a3f723bb55521ec02dda8d088427b1939721366155f5ac45dd8e1429992e7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e901ea71-aeab-5e95-939e-2401558aa708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Extrema of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb7c8a88-7f04-5f7e-9b7f-f4f5587b5967" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316033} {:answer/id #uuid "ca196cc7-5247-57ff-aab0-7f32005b1f39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316034} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c2d143-9fbc-55ff-b6d3-fb85b27cdf06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316035} {:answer/id #uuid "12b0d977-b3cb-5278-8cd6-1f04b91f46cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316036} {:answer/id #uuid "96a1a329-9bb4-5188-ae5d-6b9297be6feb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96a1a329-9bb4-5188-ae5d-6b9297be6feb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316037} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c9a0c81-1e18-52e2-b8f7-b3422105b226" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "941af544-ae4c-5a01-8911-5ecc21b5700c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79342" :question/problem "Find the global minimum value of the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1$ on the closed interval $[-1,3].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-3{x}^{2}+6x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{2}+6x & =0 \\\\ -3x(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0,2,$ and the endpoints $x=-1,3$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-(-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2}-1=3 \\\\ f(0) & =-(0)^{3}+3(0)^{2}-1=-1 \\\\ f(2) & =-(2)^{3}+3(2)^{2}-1=3 \\\\ f(3) & =-(3)^{3}+3(3)^{2}-1=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=f(2)=3,$ so the global maximum is $3.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(0)=f(3)=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/ebc8acabf87ca5c3423f2f7c07637a87c5963d9cb080a4d52f9efd87846380d1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e901ea71-aeab-5e95-939e-2401558aa708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Extrema of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e1516c2-cc07-5c5f-b898-0990f23acb1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316040} {:answer/id #uuid "433119df-86a3-5468-9111-f82c4d0d2689" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316041} {:answer/id #uuid "8503c929-858e-5499-9966-338c4d064113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316042} {:answer/id #uuid "4de72150-9af2-58c3-b28d-7d3a160b8362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316043} {:answer/id #uuid "6b2d2a4f-fa93-59c6-9188-13be7147ccaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8503c929-858e-5499-9966-338c4d064113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316042} :answer-field/id #uuid "200ef30a-708e-5701-8caa-a99a29a67914" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31376917-58ed-53d3-897e-f420b4febe17" :question/math-academy-id "q-79348" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1},$ defined on the closed interval $[-2,1],$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The global minimum value is $-1$\n- The global maximum value is $1$\n- The global maximum value is $\\frac{1}{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1({x}^{2}+1)-x(2x)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\pm 1,$ and the endpoints $x=-2,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =-\\frac{(-2)}{(-2)^{2}+1}=\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ f(-1) & =-\\frac{(-1)}{(-1)^{2}+1}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(1) & =-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}+1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=\\frac{1}{2},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(1)=-\\frac{1}{2},$ so the global minimum is $-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/f3165b8b46c8ac0ab798ae6162d4b4facb92367f5072598bef936801bce44fc4.png)"}]}
